 | 0263 | 2026-11-20 | Alloys phase P6a: the **picker's logic**, with no screen yet. `src/alloykit/picker.ts` (pure): `buildPicker` gives one row per Element with the Alloys that can do it, the features and limits each really has, what is chosen, a state (ok, unchosen, off, unknown Alloy, unsupported, missing permissions) and a plain-words warning; `choose` is the only way a selection changes (a required Element cannot be Off, the Alloy must exist and support the Element, the build policy applies); `pickerReady` and `pickerWarnings` say what blocks and what only warns; `alloysInUse` lists the Alloys whose sign-in fields Settings should show. App side: `settings.alloySelection` holds the saved choice, `src/alloyhost/savedSelection.ts` reads it (an empty object counts as never chosen), `src/alloyhost/choice.ts` saves and clears it and has `connectAlloy(id)`, which asks an Alloy to check its sign-in and never throws. `appRouter` now reads the saved choice. Nothing changes for anyone until a choice is saved: with none saved the router reads today's behaviour as before (pinned), and once one is saved an unset Element is off. Not changed on purpose: the Settings Shopify form still calls `fetchShop` directly (P6b rebuilds that screen on `connectAlloy`, which needs the connect report to carry the shop name and a detail line first). The choice is per device for now; whether it should be shared by all registers through shared state is a P6b decision. New `tests/alloypicker.test.ts`. | `src/alloykit/picker.ts`, `src/alloyhost/{savedSelection,choice,appRouter}.ts`, `src/state/store.ts`, tests |
 | 0264 | 2026-11-20 | Alloys phase P6b: the **Connections screen** (More ▸ Settings ▸ Connections, `src/screens/Connections.tsx`). One section per Element with a chip for each connection that can do it (and Off for optional ones), the features and limits of the chosen one, the plain-words warning, **Check connections** (asks each connection in use and shows what each is missing) and **Save choice**. It opens on the saved choice or else on what the app does today, so looking changes nothing; Save asks first and says no data is moved; **Go back to the current setup** removes the saved choice. Save is blocked while Catalogue, Orders or Shared state is unchosen. The Shopify form in Settings now checks the connection with `connectAlloy('shopify')` instead of reading the shop itself; the success message is word for word the same, and so is every error except one: a network failure now reads "Could not connect: Could not reach Shopify. Check the internet connection and try again." instead of the raw network message. To make that possible the connect report gained `name` (the shop name) and `detail` (the permissions line). New help page `settings/connections` (help bundle rebuilt). Still open for P6c: the reader check, pairing and the `ZellerRoot` provider move onto the Zeller Alloy; whether the choice is shared by all registers is not decided (it is per iPad). Tests added to `tests/alloypicker.test.ts`. | `src/screens/{Connections,Settings}.tsx`, `src/alloykit/{picker,types}.ts`, `src/alloys/shopify/alloy.ts`, `help/settings/connections.md`, `help/help.json`, tests |
 | 0265 | 2026-11-20 | Alloys phase P6c: the **Zeller reader hand-off** moves onto the Zeller Alloy, which finishes P6. Pay and Diagnostics check the reader with `checkTerminal()` and Diagnostics pairs it with `pairTerminal()` (both in `src/alloyhost/terminal.ts`): the check is the Alloy's `connect`, which calls the same bridge check and so updates the reader state and message exactly as before, and pairing is the Alloy's new optional `pair`. `App.tsx` mounts `AlloyHosts` (new, `src/alloyhost/AlloyHosts.tsx`) where `ZellerRoot` was: it mounts the native host of every registered Alloy that has one (only Zeller), whatever is chosen, in a fixed order worked out once, because dropping the reader host mid-payment by changing the choice would lose the payment. No screen imports the Zeller bridge now. Two small differences: a reader check that throws now comes back as a plain not-ready instead of an error, and with the Card terminal Element off, pairing says to choose a reader in Settings. Device test T120 (Connections is T119): open the app cold, take a card payment on the reader, a card refund, Diagnostics ▸ Check reader and Setup (pair terminal), and practice mode's pretend reader; all should look and behave as before. Tests in `tests/alloyp5terminal.test.ts`. | `App.tsx`, `src/alloyhost/{terminal,AlloyHosts}.ts(x)`, `src/alloys/zeller/alloy.ts`, `src/alloykit/types.ts`, `src/screens/{Pay,Diagnostics}.tsx`, tests |
+| 0266 | 2026-11-21 | Alloys phase P7a: **Pure Tillium**, a built-in Alloy (`src/alloys/tillium/alloy.ts`) that keeps the shop's data on the device with no outside service: Catalogue (items, collections, create and update), Stock (levels, adjust and set, changes since a cursor), Discounts (reads what is stored), Customers (search by name, email or phone digits, create, update, order counts), Orders (record once per sale, numbered #T1001 onward, list by tab and page, fulfil, refund with restock) and Gift cards (create, look up by a scanned code, debit and credit, safe-to-repeat versions, history, outstanding balance). It follows the contract exactly, so the till cannot tell it from Shopify: orders carry the same gateway names the refund screen reads (`Zeller`, `Cash`, `Gift card`), every write that takes a key is safe to repeat, and practice mode refuses its writes through the router like any other Alloy. It imports only types and does no network. It is **not offered yet**: its store is in memory until P7b, so `PURE_TILLIUM_READY` in `src/alloys/index.ts` is false and the picker does not list it; the conformance suite and the new `tests/alloytillium.test.ts` (11 tests, including a whole shop through the router) run it anyway. Shared state is not part of it: a Pure Tillium shop takes Shared state from iCloud (P7b). Orders under Open show everything, since there is no archive. No change to the app. | `src/alloys/tillium/alloy.ts`, `src/alloys/index.ts`, tests |
 | 0241 | 2026-11-14 | **B7e, Help pictures for three more pages (your request).** The picture engine (`src/lib/helpFigures.ts`) already drew 12 pages; this adds seven figures drawn from the real screens' labels: Cloud sync (set up, invite link, join, Back up everything and Export all events, which the page did not describe at all), Offline and sync errors (the Support & diagnostics Sync rows, with the new **Last full sync** line explained) and Split and part payments (the Split amount sheet and the Payment screen after a split). 15 of 68 pages now have a figure. No code; `help.json` rebuilt, the 63 help tests pass. Not looked at on a device: the pictures follow the screens' labels but I cannot see the iPad. The other 53 pages are not drawn; the Daily float report is the hardest (it draws notes and coins) and was left. | B7e |
 | 0240 | 2026-11-14 | **B8, review of the gift-card calls before the live test (your request).** Read `giftcards.ts` against Shopify's `giftCardCredit` and `giftCardDebit` pages: the mutations, fields and the `write_gift_card_transactions` scope are as documented. Shopify gives neither call an idempotency key, and the cancel side already looked in the card's history before crediting again, but the **charge did not**: if Shopify took the charge and the reply was lost, the app said "could not be charged", the cashier tapped again and the card was charged twice. New `debitGiftCardOnce` and `src/lib/shopify/giftCardOnce.ts`: the Nth payment of a sale has the same note on every retry (`POS sale <8 chars> tender N`), the card's history is read for it first, and a debit already there is not sent again (Shopify returns a debit as a negative amount, so amounts are compared by size). The credit side now uses the same helper. Pay.tsx charges through it. `tests/giftcardonce.test.ts` (6 tests) pass. DEVICE_TESTS T10 gains the Wi-Fi-off charge test and a check of the notes. **The live run is still yours:** it spends real gift-card value on your store. | B8 |
 | 0239 | 2026-11-11 | **iCloud was slow, and polls could pile up.** Found by reading the code, not measured on a device: the 25 second tick called `pollShared()` straight away with no check for one already running, and the change pushes and the Sync now button call it too. One poll is about twenty iCloud round trips in a row, so on a slow connection it takes longer than 25 seconds and every tick started another, all competing for iCloud and rewriting the same local state; a cash drawer closed on one iPad then took minutes to show on the other. `pollShared` is now single-flight (`src/lib/singleFlight.ts`): a call made while a poll runs joins it. Diagnostics ▸ Sync gains a **Last full sync** line with the total time and the slowest parts (`src/lib/pollTiming.ts`), so the next step is based on real numbers. Not changed, and likely still slow: the poll's steps run one after another, Back up everything and the events export read 100 records per round trip, and the iCloud tab asks iCloud four things in a row to connect. Parallelising the steps is the next lever but needs a look at what each writes first. Not run on a device. `tests/companionjobs`, `logosync` and `noticesync` cannot run in the sandbox (missing modules) and fail the same without this change; their source checks were run by hand and hold. | Sync |
